  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193353/assets/1bcb344835531289baaaad6e00823947002126f9ba7365059b53393d29d3e45d.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is given above. Find all the values of $x$ where the function $y=f(x)$ is decreasing.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is equal to zero on $(a,b).$\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is positive on $(a,b).$\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is negative on $(a,b).$\n\nFrom the above graph, we can see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-4),$ $(-2,0)$ and $(2,7):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193353/assets/601ab59d48ed791a0b468ad969a58e5a39d373e670698b3175b62eaf936204f8.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is decreasing when  $x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (-2,0)\\cup (2,7).$",
